// gsql/algorithms/community_components.gsql -- WS2. Weakly-connected
// components (WCC) over the Card-Device-Address-RecipientEmail sharing
// projection, honoring as_of (PRD sec.8.1: only edges with first_ts <= as_of
// participate). No GDS/tg_wcc is available on this TigerGraph Community
// Edition build, so this is a hand-written HASH-MIN label-propagation WCC.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two guards, one per side of the bipartite projection
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Card-to-shared-entity projection is bipartite: cards on one side,
// Device/Address/EmailDomain on the other. It has TWO independent ways of
// manufacturing a giant component, and each needs its own guard. An earlier
// revision of this query capped only the entity side, which is why a large
// residual component survived every attempt to tune that one cap.
//
// (1) ENTITY SIDE -- max_hub_degree. A shared vertex touched by an absurd
//     number of distinct cards is a missing/default sentinel value in the
//     IEEE-CIS-derived source data, not a real shared identity. Measured on
//     the full dataset: exactly 20 vertices have distinct-card degree > 1000
//     (1 Device, 14 Address, 5 EmailDomain) and those are the sentinels.
//     Any shared vertex above max_hub_degree is excluded up front.
//
//     max_hub_degree's default is deliberately LOOSE (1000 -- see
//     gsql/install.ts). Its only job is removing those 20 sentinels. An
//     earlier revision used 25, which is far too aggressive: measured on the
//     full dataset a cap of 25 discards 59% of all CARD_DEVICE edges, 98% of
//     all CARD_ADDRESS edges and 99% of all CARD_RECIPIENT_EMAIL edges,
//     throwing away nearly the entire address/email signal. Note what these
//     vertices actually are -- Address is addr1, a billing REGION (332
//     distinct values for 16,324 cards), and EmailDomain is a domain, not an
//     address (60 distinct values). Neither is ever an identifier on its own,
//     so "popular" is the normal case for them, not a defect to be filtered.
//
// (2) CARD SIDE -- min_overlap_pct, plus the 2-distinct-types rule. The
//     symmetric failure the old revision missed. The probability that two
//     cards happen to share some rare entity scales with the product of how
//     many entities each of them touches, so the most ACTIVE cards collide
//     with each other by volume alone and chain into one blob. Measured on
//     the 274-card residual the old revision produced: its members averaged
//     924 transactions and a footprint of 138.6 distinct shared entities,
//     against dataset averages of 36 and 7.8 -- a 17.8x enrichment in
//     footprint. Its confirmed-fraud rate was 40/49 = 81.6%, at or slightly
//     BELOW the 83.2% dataset-wide baseline (4,473 confirmed of 5,373 closed
//     cases with an outcome). It was a volume artifact, not a fraud ring.
//
// Adjacency therefore requires BOTH of:
//   a. the two cards share at least TWO DISTINCT ENTITY TYPES (e.g. a device
//      AND an address, not just one or the other), and
//   b. the entities they share account for at least min_overlap_pct percent
//      of their combined distinct-entity footprint (a Jaccard bar, using each
//      card's FULL uncapped footprint as the denominator -- so a card that
//      touched 600 entities is not "identified" by having 2 of them in
//      common with someone).
//
// (b) is what actually kills the volume artifact, because it scales the
// evidence bar with how promiscuous each card is. Sweeping it on the live
// graph (max_hub_degree=1000): at 15% the largest component is 721 cards, at
// 25% it is 93, at 30% it is 41. From 25% upward the largest component's
// members average a footprint of 4.9 and 12.8 transactions -- BELOW the
// dataset averages -- i.e. the largest component stops being the most active
// cards, which is the structural test that the artifact is gone.
//
// Rejected alternatives, measured rather than assumed:
//   - Requiring all THREE types instead of two: makes the artifact PURER,
//     not smaller in character. The resulting 17-card component averaged
//     4,459 transactions and a 528.8 footprint per card, and it produced
//     zero components with enough connected cases for the discovery pass.
//     Three-type overlap is simply a rarer coincidence that only the very
//     highest-volume cards can achieve.
//   - Requiring temporal proximity of the shared usage: does not
//     discriminate here. The artifact's members are active across the whole
//     six-month window (edge timestamps span 2016-07-02 to 2016-12-31), so
//     their usage intervals overlap trivially.
//   - Requiring 2+ shared INSTANCES of a type: high-volume cards clear that
//     bar easily, for the same product-of-degrees reason, so it does not
//     target the mechanism.
// See docs/decisions.md for the full write-up.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Mechanics
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Build each card's per-type neighbor sets (@viaDevice/@viaAddr/@viaEmail,
// hub-degree-capped, single-hop through the shared vertex as usual) plus a
// per-neighbor count of how many distinct shared entities back the pair
// (@shared) and each card's own uncapped footprint (@foot, published to
// @@foot so a neighbor's footprint can be looked up by id). Tally how many of
// the 3 type-sets each neighbor id appears in, keep the neighbors that clear
// both (a) and (b) as @strongNeighbors. Label propagation then runs over
// @strongNeighbors directly (a per-vertex SetAccum of card ids), pulling from
// a query-scoped id->label map (@@globalLabel) each round rather than a
// further graph hop, since @strongNeighbors is not tied to a specific edge to
// traverse. Bounded at 6 rounds (a documented simplification -- true
// convergence needs rounds >= the component's diameter in this projection;
// with the largest component now at 41 cards this is comfortable).
//
// The adjacency relation is symmetric by construction: @shared and the type
// tallies are built from the same set of shared entities from either side,
// and the @@foot lookup is symmetric, so u is a strong neighbor of v exactly
// when v is a strong neighbor of u. WCC correctness depends on that.
//
// PRINT output: per-component size summary. Not installed for writes --
// purely a computation query; discovery_report.gsql and community_lookup.gsql
// re-derive the same labels themselves (RUN QUERY composition/chaining is not
// relied upon -- unverified on this build). All three MUST be kept in sync;
// community_lookup.gsql's synthesized community id and discovery_report.gsql's
// "disc_c_<id>" Pattern id only agree because they compute the same clusters.
CREATE QUERY community_components(DATETIME as_of, INT max_hub_degree, INT min_overlap_pct) FOR GRAPH hhgoa_fraud {
  MinAccum<STRING> @label;
  SumAccum<INT>    @degree;
  SumAccum<INT>    @foot;
  SetAccum<STRING> @viaDevice;
  SetAccum<STRING> @viaAddr;
  SetAccum<STRING> @viaEmail;
  MapAccum<STRING, SumAccum<INT>> @overlapCount;
  MapAccum<STRING, SumAccum<INT>> @shared;
  SetAccum<STRING> @strongNeighbors;
  SetAccum<STRING> @srcCards; // per-hub-vertex: cards touching this device/address/email

  SumAccum<INT> @@round;
  MapAccum<STRING, SumAccum<INT>> @@comp_size;
  MapAccum<STRING, MinAccum<STRING>> @@globalLabel;
  MapAccum<STRING, MaxAccum<INT>> @@foot;

  AllCards = {Card.*};
  Init = SELECT c FROM AllCards:c
         POST-ACCUM c.@label += c.id, @@globalLabel += (c.id -> c.id);

  // Hub degree (distinct cards touching each shared vertex) and, in the same
  // scans, each card's uncapped footprint (distinct shared entities touched).
  DevAll = SELECT d FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_DEVICE:e)-:d
           WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
           ACCUM d.@degree += 1, c.@foot += 1;
  AddrAll = SELECT a FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_ADDRESS:e)-:a
            WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
            ACCUM a.@degree += 1, c.@foot += 1;
  EmAll = SELECT x FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_RECIPIENT_EMAIL:e)-:x
          WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
          ACCUM x.@degree += 1, c.@foot += 1;

  // Publish footprints by card id so a neighbor's can be read during the
  // pairwise overlap test below (vertex accums cannot be looked up by id).
  Foot = SELECT c FROM AllCards:c
         POST-ACCUM @@foot += (c.id -> c.@foot);

  // Per-type neighbor sets, hub-degree-capped, with per-neighbor counts of
  // how many distinct shared entities back each pair.
  DevHub = SELECT d FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_DEVICE:e)-:d
           WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of AND d.@degree <= max_hub_degree
           ACCUM d.@srcCards += c.id;
  DevBack = SELECT c2 FROM DevHub:d-(reverse_CARD_DEVICE:e)-:c2
            WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
            ACCUM
              FOREACH src IN d.@srcCards DO
                IF src != c2.id THEN
                  c2.@viaDevice += src,
                  c2.@shared += (src -> 1)
                END
              END;

  AddrHub = SELECT a FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_ADDRESS:e)-:a
            WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of AND a.@degree <= max_hub_degree
            ACCUM a.@srcCards += c.id;
  AddrBack = SELECT c2 FROM AddrHub:a-(reverse_CARD_ADDRESS:e)-:c2
             WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
             ACCUM
               FOREACH src IN a.@srcCards DO
                 IF src != c2.id THEN
                   c2.@viaAddr += src,
                   c2.@shared += (src -> 1)
                 END
               END;

  EmHub = SELECT x FROM AllCards:c-(CARD_RECIPIENT_EMAIL:e)-:x
          WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of AND x.@degree <= max_hub_degree
          ACCUM x.@srcCards += c.id;
  EmBack = SELECT c2 FROM EmHub:x-(reverse_CARD_RECIPIENT_EMAIL:e)-:c2
           WHERE e.first_ts <= as_of
           ACCUM
             FOREACH src IN x.@srcCards DO
               IF src != c2.id THEN
                 c2.@viaEmail += src,
                 c2.@shared += (src -> 1)
               END
             END;

  // Tally per-neighbor type-overlap; keep neighbors that share >= 2 distinct
  // types AND whose shared entities are >= min_overlap_pct of the pair's
  // combined footprint. Integer form of
  //   shared / (foot(c) + foot(nb) - shared) >= min_overlap_pct / 100.
  Overlap = SELECT c FROM AllCards:c
            ACCUM
              FOREACH id1 IN c.@viaDevice DO c.@overlapCount += (id1 -> 1) END,
              FOREACH id2 IN c.@viaAddr DO c.@overlapCount += (id2 -> 1) END,
              FOREACH id3 IN c.@viaEmail DO c.@overlapCount += (id3 -> 1) END
            POST-ACCUM
              FOREACH (nb, cnt) IN c.@overlapCount DO
                IF cnt >= 2 AND
                   c.@shared.get(nb) * 100 >=
                     min_overlap_pct * (c.@foot + @@foot.get(nb) - c.@shared.get(nb)) THEN
                  c.@strongNeighbors += nb
                END
              END;

  // Hash-min propagation over @strongNeighbors (no further graph hop needed).
  WHILE @@round < 6 DO
    Prop = SELECT c FROM AllCards:c
           ACCUM
             FOREACH nb IN c.@strongNeighbors DO
               c.@label += @@globalLabel.get(nb)
             END
           POST-ACCUM @@globalLabel += (c.id -> c.@label);
    @@round += 1;
  END;

  Summ = SELECT c FROM AllCards:c
         ACCUM @@comp_size += (c.@label -> 1);

  PRINT @@comp_size AS component_sizes;
}
